# Response Header and Model Mapping Migration Workflow

This record originated from a compatibility review of Compat Relay 2.19. At first, two things needed to be examined together: headers that appeared repeatedly in the response, and the model mapping that callers depended on. They were grouped because both are easily treated as ordinary fields during migration; there is currently no evidence that they should follow the same fix path.

The old investigation started at `src/Google/Service/Resource.php`. That location contains `Google_Service_Resource`, and the file header imports `GuzzleHttp\Psr7\Request`. Keep this as a reference point: first confirm the resource object through which the request and response pass, then check whether the caller treats the same-named header as a single value. Do not replace the entire call chain just because you have seen the class name.

The working assumption at the time was: after normalizing the name, take one usable value first; if duplicate values appear in the observed case, retain the raw response and let the caller decide. This was to avoid immediately changing existing read logic, not a confirmed long-term format. The model mapping followed the same conservative handling: retain the old mapping first, then select a caller still in use to verify the version and entry point.

The migration order is provisionally:

1. Pin down an existing test or example that reliably produces duplicate headers;
2. Confirm the request-adjacent location from the resource object;
3. Record whether the caller reads a single value or the raw response;
4. Separately verify the actual version of the model mapping;
5. If the old caller cannot be explained, first fall back to the pre-migration read behavior.

Here, "fall back" means only that the caller continues reading according to the old interpretation; it does not mean making a global rewrite within `Resource.php`. Both the duplicate headers and the mapping differences still lack a durable counterexample.
